Confluence Wiki Markup Writing Guide for AI Agents

1. Purpose

Use this guide whenever you are asked to generate content in Confluence Wiki Markup format.

Your output must be ready to paste into a system that accepts Confluence Wiki Markup.

Do not output Markdown unless the user explicitly requests Markdown.

Do not wrap the final output in a Markdown code fence unless the user explicitly asks for a code block.

---

2. Important Compatibility Note

Confluence Wiki Markup is primarily supported by:

- Confluence Legacy Editor
- Some Confluence macros
- Some administration fields
- Jira or Confluence integrations that explicitly accept Wiki Markup
- APIs or automation tools that expect Wiki Markup

The modern Confluence Cloud editor may not accept raw Wiki Markup directly.

Before generating content, determine which format the target system expects:

- Confluence Wiki Markup
- Confluence Storage Format
- Markdown
- HTML
- Atlassian Document Format, also called ADF

When the user explicitly requests Confluence Wiki Format, use the syntax described in this document.

---

3. General Output Rules

When generating Confluence Wiki Markup:

1. Use "h1.", "h2.", "h3." and similar syntax for headings.
2. Use "*text*" for bold text.
3. Use "_text_" for italic text.
4. Use "{{text}}" for inline code.
5. Use "{code}" for code blocks.
6. Use "||" for table header cells.
7. Use "|" for normal table cells.
8. Use "*" for unordered lists.
9. Use "#" for ordered lists.
10. Use Confluence-style links such as "[label|URL]".
11. Use macros only when they are supported by the target Confluence installation.
12. Keep spacing consistent.
13. Add blank lines between major sections.
14. Avoid mixing Markdown syntax with Confluence Wiki Markup.
15. Return only valid Confluence Wiki Markup when the user asks for paste-ready output.

---

4. Headings

Use the following syntax:

{noformat}
h1. Main Heading

h2. Section Heading

h3. Subsection Heading

h4. Smaller Heading

h5. Minor Heading

h6. Smallest Heading
{noformat}

Example:

{noformat}
h1. Deployment Guide

h2. Prerequisites

h3. Required Permissions
{noformat}

Heading Rules

- Use only one "h1." heading for the page title when a page title is required.
- Use "h2." for major sections.
- Use "h3." for subsections.
- Do not skip heading levels without a reason.
- Add one space after the heading marker.

Correct:

{noformat}
h2. Installation
{noformat}

Incorrect:

{noformat}
h2.Installation

Installation

Installation
{noformat}

