11. Tables

Basic Table

Use double pipes for header cells and single pipes for normal cells.

Example:

{noformat}
|| Field || Required || Description ||
| Name | Yes | Name of the service |
| Owner | Yes | Team responsible for the service |
| Repository | Yes | Git repository URL |
| Notes | No | Additional information |
{noformat}

Table Rules

- Every row must start and end with "|".
- Header cells use "||".
- Normal cells use "|".
- Keep the number of cells consistent across rows.
- Keep cell content concise.
- Avoid very large paragraphs inside tables.
- Use a list instead of a table when the information does not require comparison.

Correct:

{noformat}
|| Environment || URL || Deployment Method ||
| Development | {{https://dev.example.com}} | Automatic |
| Testing | {{https://test.example.com}} | Automatic |
| Production | {{https://example.com}} | Manual approval |
{noformat}

Incorrect:

{noformat}
| Environment | URL
Development | example.com
{noformat}

Lists Inside Tables

For simple lists inside a table cell, keep the list on the same logical row.

Example:

{noformat}
|| Component || Responsibilities ||
| API | * Request validation

* Business logic
* Database access |
  | Worker | * Message processing
* Retry handling
* Status updates |
  {noformat}

Be aware that support for complex formatting inside table cells may vary.

For maximum compatibility, use short text or "<br>" only when the target system supports it.

---

12. Code Blocks

Use the "{code}" macro.

Basic Code Block

{noformat}
{code}
const message = "Hello";
console.log(message);
{code}
{noformat}

Code Block With Language

{noformat}
{code:language=javascript}
const message = "Hello";
console.log(message);
{code}
{noformat}

Code Block With Title

{noformat}
{code:language=javascript|title=server.js}
import Koa from "koa";

const app = new Koa();
app.listen(3000);
{code}
{noformat}

Code Block With Line Numbers

{noformat}
{code:language=javascript|linenumbers=true}
function add(a, b) {
return a + b;
}
{code}
{noformat}

Recommended Language Names

Use common identifiers such as:

- "javascript"
- "typescript"
- "java"
- "python"
- "bash"
- "shell"
- "sql"
- "json"
- "yaml"
- "xml"
- "html"
- "css"
- "docker"
- "text"

If syntax highlighting is uncertain, omit the language parameter.

Code Block Rules

- Do not use Markdown triple backticks.
- Do not apply bold or italic formatting inside a code block.
- Preserve indentation.
- Do not escape code unless required.
- Use inline code for short commands.
- Use a code block for multiline commands or source code.

---

13. No-Format Blocks

Use "{noformat}" when text must appear exactly as written but does not require syntax highlighting.

Example:

{noformat}
{noformat}
SERVICE_NAME=orders-api
PORT=3000
LOG_LEVEL=info
{noformat}
{noformat}

Use "{noformat}" for:

- Plain configuration examples
- Log output
- Directory trees
- Console output
- Text templates
- Content containing many Wiki Markup characters

Example directory tree:

{noformat}
{noformat}
project/
├── apps/
│   ├── api/
│   └── web/
├── packages/
└── package.json
{noformat}
{noformat}

---

14. Quotes

Use "bq." for a block quote.

Example:

{noformat}
bq. All production changes must be reviewed before deployment.
{noformat}

For multiple quoted paragraphs, use the quote macro:

{noformat}
{quote}
The first quoted paragraph.

The second quoted paragraph.
{quote}
{noformat}

Use quotes only for actual quotations, policies or highlighted statements.

---

15. Horizontal Rules

Use four hyphens:

{noformat}

{noformat}

Example:

{noformat}
h2. Current Design

Content describing the current design.

---

h2. Proposed Design

Content describing the proposed design.
{noformat}

Use horizontal rules sparingly.

Headings and whitespace are usually preferable.

---

16. Information Panels

Confluence commonly supports panel macros for highlighted information.

Info Panel

{noformat}
{info}
This process applies only to production deployments.
{info}
{noformat}

Warning Panel

{noformat}
{warning}
Do not delete the production database.
{warning}
{noformat}

Note Panel

{noformat}
{note}
The first deployment may take several minutes.
{note}
{noformat}

Tip Panel

{noformat}
{tip}
Run the tests locally before opening a pull request.
{tip}
{noformat}

Custom Panel

{noformat}
{panel:title=Important Information}
This is the panel content.
{panel}
{noformat}

Depending on the target Confluence version, supported panel macros may vary.

When compatibility is uncertain, use a bold label instead:

{noformat}
Warning: Do not deploy this change before the database migration is complete.
{noformat}

---

17. Expandable Sections

When supported, use the expand macro.

Example:

{noformat}
{expand:title=View implementation details}
The implementation uses a background worker and a MongoDB-backed queue.
{expand}
{noformat}

Use expandable sections for:

- Long technical details
- Troubleshooting information
- Large examples
- Optional background information
- Logs
- Secondary explanations

Do not hide critical instructions inside an expandable section.

---

18. Table of Contents

Use the table of contents macro:

{noformat}
{toc}
{noformat}

Example:

{noformat}
h1. Service Deployment Guide

{toc}

h2. Overview

h2. Prerequisites

h2. Deployment

h2. Verification

h2. Rollback
{noformat}

Optional parameters may be added:

{noformat}
{toc:minLevel=2|maxLevel=3}
{noformat}

Use a table of contents for long pages with several sections.

Do not add one to very short pages.

---

19. Status and Labels

Some Confluence environments support status macros.

Example:

{noformat}
{status:colour=Green|title=COMPLETED}
{noformat}

Other examples:

{noformat}
{status:colour=Yellow|title=IN PROGRESS}

{status:colour=Red|title=BLOCKED}

{status:colour=Blue|title=PLANNED}
{noformat}

Macro parameters can vary between Confluence versions.

When exact compatibility is unknown, use plain text:

{noformat}
Status: In Progress
{noformat}

---

20. Images and Attachments

Attached Image

Syntax:

{noformat}
!architecture.png!
{noformat}

Image With Size

Example:

{noformat}
!architecture.png|width=800!
{noformat}

External Image

Example:

{noformat}
!https://example.com/image.png!
{noformat}

External image support may be restricted by security settings.

Link to an Attachment

Example:

{noformat}
[^deployment-guide.pdf]
{noformat}

Link With Custom Text

Example:

{noformat}
[Download the deployment guide^deployment-guide.pdf]
{noformat}

Do not invent attachment names.

Only reference an attachment when its name is known.

