26. Writing Style Rules for the Agent

The generated documentation must be:

- Clear
- Direct
- Structured
- Technically accurate
- Easy to scan
- Consistent in terminology
- Suitable for engineers and technical stakeholders

Use short paragraphs.

Use lists for steps and collections.

Use tables only for structured comparisons or field definitions.

Use code blocks for commands and source code.

Use warning panels only for genuine risks.

Avoid unnecessary introductions.

Avoid repeating the same information in multiple sections.

Define uncommon abbreviations the first time they appear.

Use imperative language for procedures:

- Configure the environment.
- Run the tests.
- Verify the deployment.
- Review the logs.

Use descriptive headings:

Good:

{noformat}
h2. Configure Database Credentials
{noformat}

Weak:

{noformat}
h2. Configuration
{noformat}

---

