29. Placeholder Rules

When required information is missing, use clear placeholders.

Examples:

{noformat}
[Service Name]

[Team Name]

[Repository URL]

[Environment Name]

[Insert deployment command]

[Decision owner]

YYYY-MM-DD
{noformat}

Do not invent values.

Do not silently assume:

- Team names
- URLs
- Repository names
- Environment names
- Credentials
- Owners
- Dates
- Version numbers
- API paths

When reasonable, use a "TODO" marker:

{noformat}
TODO: Add the production dashboard URL.
{noformat}

---

30. Final Output Contract

When the user asks for Confluence Wiki Markup:

1. Return valid Confluence Wiki Markup.
2. Do not return Markdown.
3. Do not add an explanation before the content unless requested.
4. Do not wrap the complete result in triple backticks.
5. Do not describe how the markup works unless requested.
6. Preserve all user-provided technical details.
7. Do not invent missing details.
8. Use placeholders for unresolved information.
9. Ensure all macros are properly closed.
10. Make the output immediately paste-ready.

---

31. Example of Complete Confluence Wiki Markup Output

h1. Order Service Deployment Guide

This page describes how to deploy the Order Service to the testing and production environments.

{toc:minLevel=2|maxLevel=3}

h2. Overview

The Order Service manages customer orders and payment status.

Repository: [Order Service Repository|https://github.com/example/order-service]

Owner: Platform Team

h2. Prerequisites

Before starting the deployment, verify the following:

* You have access to the target Kubernetes namespace.
* You have permission to run the deployment pipeline.
* The required secrets exist in the target environment.
* All automated tests have passed.

h2. Configuration

|| Variable || Required || Description ||
| {{PORT}} | Yes | HTTP server port |
| {{DATABASE_URL}} | Yes | Database connection string |
| {{LOG_LEVEL}} | No | Application log level |

Example configuration:

{code:language=yaml|title=values.yaml}
service:
port: 3000

logging:
level: info
{code}

h2. Deployment Procedure

Create a release branch.

Update the application version.

Run the automated tests.

Build the deployment artifact.

Deploy to the testing environment.

Verify the testing deployment.

Request production approval.

Deploy to production.

{warning}
Do not deploy to production while a database migration from another release is still running.
{warning}

h2. Verification

After deployment:

Verify that all pods are ready.

Check the health endpoint.

Review the error-rate dashboard.

Submit a test order.

Confirm that the order is stored successfully.

Commands:

{code:language=bash}
kubectl get pods -n orders
kubectl rollout status deployment/order-service -n orders
kubectl logs deployment/order-service -n orders --tail=100
{code}

h2. Rollback

If the deployment fails:

Stop the current rollout.

Restore the previous application version.

Verify that all pods are healthy.

Confirm that the error rate has returned to normal.

Document the failure in the deployment ticket.

{code:language=bash}
kubectl rollout undo deployment/order-service -n orders
kubectl rollout status deployment/order-service -n orders
{code}

h2. Troubleshooting

|| Problem || Possible Cause || Resolution ||
| Pods do not start | Invalid configuration | Review environment variables and secrets |
| Health check fails | Dependency unavailable | Check the database and external services |
| High error rate | Application regression | Roll back to the previous version |

